# Stop the HDMI CEC devices pretending to be a mouse.
#
# Installed to /etc/udev/rules.d/ by setup_kiosk.sh.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THIS, NOT THE CURSOR THEME, IS WHAT REMOVES THE ARROW FROM THE SCREEN
#
# The rig has no mouse, yet an arrow sat in the dead centre of the 5.5" panel.
# Three attempts at a transparent cursor THEME did nothing, and the reason is
# that the arrow was never a theme cursor at all -- it was chromium's own
# built-in pointer bitmap, drawn as a client cursor surface.
#
# Where the pointer came from:
#
#     $ cat /proc/bus/input/devices
#     N: Name="vc4-hdmi-0"     Bus=001e (BUS_CEC)     B: EV=100017
#     N: Name="vc4-hdmi-1"     Bus=001e (BUS_CEC)     B: EV=100017
#
# Those are the HDMI CEC remote-control endpoints the vc4 driver registers for
# each HDMI port. EV=0x100017 has bit 2 set -- EV_REL, relative axes -- so
# libinput classifies them as POINTERS:
#
#     $ libinput list-devices
#     Device: WaveShare WaveShare   Capabilities: touch
#     Device: vc4-hdmi-0            Capabilities: keyboard pointer   <-- here
#     Device: vc4-hdmi-1            Capabilities: keyboard pointer   <-- here
#
# The touchscreen is touch-only and innocent. These two are the entire reason
# a cursor exists on a machine with no mouse.
#
# WHY CSS cursor:none COULD NOT FIX IT
# chromium only recomputes the cursor for the element under the pointer when it
# receives a pointer EVENT. A phantom CEC pointer never moves: chromium set its
# default arrow once, when the pointer entered the surface, and had no reason
# to look at the page's `cursor:none` ever again. The rule was applied and
# correct the whole time and could never take effect.
#
# So the fix is upstream of all of it -- have no pointer. With these ignored,
# the seat has no pointer capability, chromium is never sent a pointer-enter,
# and nothing draws a cursor.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS: nothing. There is no CEC remote on this rig, and if one is
# ever wanted, delete this file. The touchscreen is a separate USB HID device
# and is not matched here. A real USB keyboard or mouse plugged in for
# debugging is also unaffected -- and would then get a working cursor, which is
# what you would want at that moment.
#
# VERIFY:
#     libinput list-devices | grep -A1 vc4-hdmi     # should list neither
#     udevadm test /sys/class/input/event1 2>&1 | grep LIBINPUT_IGNORE_DEVICE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# "vc4-hdmi-?" matches vc4-hdmi-0 and vc4-hdmi-1 but NOT the separate
# "vc4-hdmi-0 HDMI Jack" switch devices, which are harmless and left alone.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{name}=="vc4-hdmi-?", ENV{LIBINPUT_IGNORE_DEVICE}="1"
